#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd806d5-cac7-5ab8-a47e-d04d92de858c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262912} {:answer/id #uuid "3d044d43-04b6-5470-b760-526c2119b7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262913} {:answer/id #uuid "feab9798-b8b0-5866-8c2e-cfeb09826f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262914} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2a0c28-1458-5844-8c84-fc64358082b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262915} {:answer/id #uuid "d51b0b64-14ee-52f3-8b05-aef2d9f3bc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d51b0b64-14ee-52f3-8b05-aef2d9f3bc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916} :answer-field/id #uuid "0036bd73-610c-537a-a14e-f81117e7e6e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ced084c-e5cc-510a-ae57-0a31b5977172" :question/math-academy-id "q-112877" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x+9?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $9$ and the leading coefficient is $-6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }9}{\\text{factor of }-6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2$ does not take the above form. The value $-2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{-1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7221697-c245-573c-be7d-652163f6e37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254323} {:answer/id #uuid "8b882184-5e97-5056-9ec0-a1434002a13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254324} {:answer/id #uuid "10d7958e-1868-5d93-8a9d-23272d7e1433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254325} {:answer/id #uuid "fa815d57-dd6d-544f-bee3-ecf188611a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254326} {:answer/id #uuid "5b605b48-1f56-5b52-a0a4-da41b6e648f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7221697-c245-573c-be7d-652163f6e37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254323} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be6948b-0bbe-50e4-8e11-a063db8462a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6369285e-2dff-5b5c-a6d1-64b6011c7055" :question/math-academy-id "q-112960" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{3}-x-2,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}]}
